.TH _PRINTF 3 "March 2025" "Holberton School" "Library Functions Manual"

.SH NAME
_printf \- simplified reproduction of the C standard printf function

.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "main.h"

int _printf(const char *format, ...);
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B _printf
function produces output according to a
.I format
string. It writes the output to the standard output (file descriptor 1).

This simplified version supports a limited set of format specifiers.
Additional arguments are processed using variadic functions
(\fBva_start\fP, \fBva_arg\fP, \fBva_end\fP).

.SH SUPPORTED FORMAT SPECIFIERS
The following conversions are supported:

.TP
.B %c
Print a single character.  
Argument type: \fBint\fP.

.TP
.B %s
Print a null-terminated string.  
Argument type: \fBchar *\fP.  
If the argument is NULL, the string "(null)" is printed.

.TP
.B %d
Print a signed decimal integer.  
Argument type: \fBint\fP.

.TP
.B %i
Same as %d.

.TP
.B %%
Print a literal percent sign.

.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
.B _printf
returns the total number of characters written.

On error (for example, if the format string is NULL or ends with a single
percent sign), the function returns \-1.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
_printf("Hello %s!\\n", "world");
  -> prints "Hello world!"

_printf("Value: %d", 42);
  -> prints "Value: 42"

_printf("Percent: %%");
  -> prints "%"
.fi

.SH NOTES
This implementation does not handle:
.IP \[bu] 4
Flags (e.g., +, \-, 0)
.IP \[bu] 4
Field width
.IP \[bu] 4
Precision
.IP \[bu] 4
Length modifiers (l, h, etc.)
.IP \[bu] 4
Floating point formats

.SH SEE ALSO
printf(3), putchar(3), write(2)

